---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "XTERRA Treadmill Troubleshooting: Power, Belt and Console";
const description =
  "XTERRA treadmill not working? Check the safety key, front breaker and a 15-amp grounded outlet first. Then the E0 to E7 codes, belt slip, tracking and LUBE.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Fault Diagnosis", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/problems/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "XTERRA Treadmill Troubleshooting", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/xterra-treadmill-troubleshooting/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my XTERRA treadmill not turning on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Check the safety key first, then the power switch and the circuit breaker on the front of the base, which you push in until it locks. Then check the wall outlet and your breaker panel. XTERRA's manuals ask for a grounded 120-volt, 15-amp outlet with no other appliances on the circuit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What does E1 mean on an XTERRA treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on the model. On the TR65, E1 means a missing speed signal, so check the speed sensor connection. On the TRX1000, TRX1200 and TR150, E1 appears 8 to 10 seconds after pressing Start, and the manual says to check whether the motor runs and the cable between the console and controller.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I reset my XTERRA treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Remove the safety key, turn the power switch off, unplug for about a minute, then plug in, switch on and replace the key. If it is completely dead, push in the circuit breaker on the front of the base. Pressing Stop twice also returns the console to its start-up screen.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I get rid of the LUBE message on my XTERRA?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Check the deck and lubricate it if it is dry. Then, on the TRX1000, TRX1200 and TR65, hold the PROGRAM key for 3 seconds in standby mode to clear the message. Some models, such as the TR75, reset it from the Engineering Mode menu instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should I lubricate an XTERRA treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Follow your model's manual. The TR65, TR75, TR95H and TRX1200 manuals say every 90 days or 90 hours, the TRX1000 manual says 80 hours, and XTERRA's FAQ says every 3 months or 180 hours. Lubricate sooner if the deck feels dry, and use only XTERRA-approved lubricant.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my XTERRA treadmill keep tripping the breaker?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually because something else shares the circuit, or because the deck is dry and the motor is working too hard. XTERRA's manuals say AFCI and GFCI breakers can trip from the treadmill's inrush current and recommend making the treadmill the only device on the circuit. Clean and lubricate the deck as well.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the XTERRA customer service number?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "XTERRA's website listed 870-336-4286, Monday to Friday 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM, on 24 November 2026. Have your model, the 16-character serial number from the frame near the power cord, and your proof of purchase ready.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-24",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/xterra-treadmill-troubleshooting/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "problems");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "xterra treadmill troubleshooting",
      "xterra treadmill not working",
      "xterra treadmill error codes",
      "xterra treadmill e1 error",
      "xterra treadmill belt slipping",
      "xterra treadmill lube message",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Find Your Symptom", link: "#short-answer" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/problems/">Fault Diagnosis</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Troubleshooting</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">XTERRA Treadmill Troubleshooting: Power, Belt, Error Codes and Console</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Most XTERRA faults come down to a short list: a safety key that is not seated, a tripped breaker on the front of the base, a circuit shared with other appliances, a dry deck, a belt that needs tension or tracking, or a LUBE reminder that needs clearing. Here is the order to check them in, what XTERRA’s own manuals say each error code means, and what to have ready before you call.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer manuals and service guidance, and the faults owners report most.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">Find Your Symptom</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          XTERRA treadmills are made by Dyaco, the Taiwanese manufacturer that also owns Spirit Fitness,
          and they share a family of simple LCD consoles, motor controllers and belt adjustments. That
          makes them easier to troubleshoot than most: there is no touchscreen operating system to freeze,
          and the owner&rsquo;s manuals XTERRA hosts on its own site spell out the error codes, the belt
          adjustments and the electrical rules in plain terms. This guide is built on those manuals and on
          XTERRA&rsquo;s support pages, read on 24 November 2026. We have not tested an XTERRA treadmill.
          Where a step or a code differs between models, we say so, and you should treat your own
          model&rsquo;s manual as the final word.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="Jump to">
          <h2>Jump to</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#wont-turn-on">XTERRA Treadmill Will Not Turn On</a></li>
            <li><a href="#breaker-and-power">Tripping Breakers, Extension Cords and Low Voltage</a></li>
            <li><a href="#error-codes">XTERRA Error Codes E0 to E7</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-slipping">Belt Slipping, Hesitating or Stopping Hard</a></li>
            <li><a href="#tracking">Belt Drifting to One Side: Tracking an XTERRA</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lubrication">Lubrication and the LUBE Message</a></li>
            <li><a href="#console-speed-incline">Console, Speed Sensor and Incline Problems</a></li>
            <li><a href="#support">When to Call XTERRA and What to Have Ready</a></li>
            <li><a href="#prevention">A Simple XTERRA Maintenance Routine</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Seat the safety key, check the power switch and the circuit breaker on the front of the
            base, and make sure the treadmill is plugged straight into a grounded 120-volt, 15-amp outlet
            with nothing else on that circuit. Then do a clean power cycle.</strong> That clears most
            XTERRA problems. If it does not, match your symptom below.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Dead console:</strong> safety key, <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a>, front breaker, wall outlet, then the breaker panel.</li>
            <li><strong>Error code on the screen:</strong> E0 points at the safety key, E2 at belt friction or overload, E5 at the console cable, E7 at the power supply (on most current models; the TR95H uses its own codes).</li>
            <li><strong>Belt slips:</strong> clean and lubricate the deck first, then tighten both rear bolts a quarter turn at a time.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt drifts to one side:</strong> adjust the rear roller bolt a quarter turn at 3 mph and wait.</li>
            <li><strong>&ldquo;LUBE&rdquo; on the display:</strong> lubricate if the deck is dry, then hold PROGRAM for 3 seconds in standby to clear it.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Symptom</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Most likely cause</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">First fix</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Completely dead</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key, front breaker, outlet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Seat the key; push the front breaker in until it locks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console on, belt will not start</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key, E0 or E1 code</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseat the key; read the code in the table below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Trips the house breaker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shared circuit, AFCI/GFCI, dry deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Give it its own circuit; clean and lubricate</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Will not go above about 5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low voltage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove the extension cord; have the voltage checked</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slips or hesitates</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck, then low tension</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate, then a quarter turn on both bolts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt runs off-centre</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tracking, uneven gait</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Quarter-turn tracking adjustment at 3 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console shuts off when touched</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Static, poor grounding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the outlet is properly grounded</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            If anything smells hot, the belt keeps running after you pull the safety key, or a breaker
            trips the moment the belt starts, stop using the treadmill and call XTERRA. For the same
            checks on any brand, see our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wont-turn-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>XTERRA Treadmill Will Not Turn On</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA&rsquo;s Sport series manuals list five causes for a display that does not light, in
            this order: the safety tether is not in place, the circuit breaker on the front of the treadmill
            has tripped, the plug is not fully in the wall outlet, the breaker in your home&rsquo;s panel has
            tripped, and finally a fault in the treadmill itself. Work through them in that order; the first
            four cost nothing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. The safety key</h3>
          <p>
            Every current XTERRA uses a magnetic safety key on a tether that clips to your clothing. The
            TR75 manual says the treadmill will not power on without it; on the TRX1200 the manual says the
            display shows E0 in the speed window if the key is missing. Either way, a key pulled half off
            by the cord looks like a dead machine. Press it firmly onto the round metal pad on the console.
            If it still is not recognised, our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety key that is not working</a> covers the switch behind it. If yours is lost, our <a href="/best-treadmill-safety-keys/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety key replacement guide</a> treats a 1-inch universal magnetic key as the fallback when you cannot match the part number.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. The power switch and front breaker</h3>
          <p>
            The main power switch is at the front of the base, below the motor hood. The Sport series
            manuals also show a circuit breaker on the front of the treadmill; when it trips, the fix is to
            push it in until it locks. Switch off and unplug before you touch it. One trip after a long,
            hard session is not unusual. A breaker that keeps tripping is a symptom, and the usual causes
            are below.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. The outlet and the circuit</h3>
          <p>
            Plug a lamp into the same socket to prove it is live, and check your breaker panel. XTERRA&rsquo;s
            manuals ask for a properly grounded 120-volt, 15-amp outlet, and the TRX1000 manual says the
            treadmill should be the only appliance on that circuit. If the console is still dark after all
            of this, our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that won&rsquo;t turn on</a> works through the cord and the controller.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A clean power cycle</h3>
          <p>
            XTERRA consoles have no software to restore the way an <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>iFIT</a> or JRNY touchscreen does, so the
            reset that matters is a clean restart: remove the safety key, turn the power switch off, unplug
            the cord, wait about a minute, then plug in, switch on and replace the key. The TR75 manual
            says all the display lights come on briefly at power-up and the console shows its software
            version before going to idle; that start-up sequence is a useful sign the console itself is
            alive. Note that some XTERRA consoles go to sleep after a period of inactivity (the TRX1000
            manual says 30 minutes; the TR75 manual describes a sleep setting of 15 minutes), so a dark
            screen on a machine nobody has touched may simply be asleep. Press a key before assuming it
            has failed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="breaker-and-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Tripping Breakers, Extension Cords and Low Voltage</h2>
<p>
            Power problems cause more XTERRA service calls than broken parts, and the manuals are unusually
            specific about them. Three rules cover most cases.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Give the treadmill its own circuit.</strong> XTERRA&rsquo;s manuals say AFCI and GFCI
            breakers may trip occasionally because of the high inrush current of the drive electronics and
            motor, and that this affects every treadmill brand. They tell you to avoid those breakers if
            possible, and, where local code requires them, to check what else is on the circuit: the
            manuals name fluorescent lights with electronic ballasts, a coffee maker, a space heater and a
            hair dryer as examples. XTERRA says it has tested several AFCI/GFCI breakers with its treadmills
            and that they did not trip as long as nothing else was plugged into the same circuit. If your
            treadmill trips a breaker in the panel, start there. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that trips the breaker</a> goes further.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid extension cords.</strong> The TRX1000 manual says not to use an <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>extension cord</a> unless it is 14 AWG or heavier with a single outlet on the end. The Sport series manuals go
            further: do not use one, and if you must, keep it as short as possible and at least 14 gauge.
            Never use a power strip, and never defeat the grounding pin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Watch the voltage.</strong> XTERRA&rsquo;s Sport series troubleshooting table has a
            telling entry: a treadmill that only reaches about 5 mph while the display shows a higher
            speed. Its explanation is low AC voltage, and its fix is to remove the extension cord and have
            an electrician or your dealer check the supply, with a minimum of 120 volts required. The
            manuals also say that if voltage varies by 10% or more, performance may suffer, and that such
            conditions are not covered under warranty.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What happens</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What XTERRA&rsquo;s manuals point to</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">House breaker trips, treadmill breaker does not</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Other appliances on the circuit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Make the treadmill the only load on that circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill&rsquo;s own 15-amp breaker trips</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High belt and deck friction</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clean the deck and lubricate it</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tops out near 5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low AC voltage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove the extension cord; check voltage</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console shuts off when touched on a cold day</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Static and poor grounding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirm the outlet is properly grounded</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt stops very abruptly when the key is pulled</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High belt and deck friction</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clean the deck and lubricate it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The static entry deserves a note. XTERRA&rsquo;s troubleshooting table says a console that
            shuts off when touched on a cold day is static electricity &ldquo;crashing&rdquo; the computer
            because the treadmill may not be grounded. A grounded outlet and a humidifier in a dry room
            help; our guide to <a href="/treadmill-static-shock/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill static shock</a> covers mats and other fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="error-codes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>XTERRA Error Codes E0 to E7</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Most current XTERRA folding treadmills use the same E0 to E7 codes, but the exact
            meaning of E1 differs between models, and the TR95H Hiker uses a different set.</strong> The
            table below is taken from the TRX1000 and TR65 owner&rsquo;s manuals on XTERRA&rsquo;s site
            (read 24 November 2026); the TRX1200 and TR150 manuals list the same codes as the TRX1000.
            Look up the code in your own model&rsquo;s manual before you order a part.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Code</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Meaning in XTERRA&rsquo;s manuals</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Owner checks</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety switch malfunction</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Seat the safety key; check the key and its harness for damage</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRX1000, TRX1200, TR150: shown 8 to 10 seconds after Start. TR65: missing speed signal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Does the motor run? Check the console-to-controller cable; on the TR65, the speed sensor connection</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over-current protection: the treadmill is overloaded</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check belt alignment; clean and lubricate the deck; let an overheated motor cool</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR65: incline error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check for anything blocking the incline; then service</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Improper motor input voltage: motor not connected properly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Service: motor-to-controller connection</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Communication lost between console and controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">With the power off, check the console cable connections</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller malfunction</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Service: controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E7</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Abnormal power input</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the outlet, circuit and voltage; remove any extension cord</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The TR95H Hiker is different</h3>
          <p>
            The TR95H manual uses its own short list. It describes E1 as the computer not reading the
            sensor signal for 7 seconds after the machine starts, E3 as a poorly connected or damaged cable
            between the computer and the control box, and E6 as the computer not reading the incline
            motor&rsquo;s position signal for 6 seconds. So an E6 on a TR95H is an incline problem, while
            on a TRX1000 it is a controller fault. This is exactly why a generic error-code table can send
            you after the wrong part.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What you can do and what you should not</h3>
          <p>
            The owner checks in the table are the ones that need no tools beyond a screwdriver for the
            motor hood: the safety key, the cable connections behind the console, the outlet and the deck.
            The manuals&rsquo; fixes for E4 and E6, and the deeper E1 and E2 causes such as a failed speed
            sensor, damaged roller bearings or an overheated motor, are parts replacements. Unplug before
            removing any cover, as every XTERRA manual insists, and if the code survives a clean power
            cycle and the owner checks, call XTERRA with the code and the moment it appears.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-slipping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Slipping, Hesitating or Stopping Hard</h2>
<p>
            <strong>On an XTERRA, a belt that slips or hesitates under each step is most often a dry or
            dirty deck, and only then a loose belt.</strong> Friction between the belt and the deck rises as
            lubricant wears off and dirt builds up, and the manuals warn that this puts stress on the drive
            motor, the drive belt and the motor control board. It is also the likely cause of E2 codes and
            of a treadmill that trips its own breaker. So check the deck first.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Then adjust the tension</h3>
          <p>
            The TRX1000 manual&rsquo;s procedure is typical of the range. The two tension bolts are at the
            rear roller, at the ends of the step rails, and take the Allen wrench supplied with the
            treadmill (6 mm on the TRX1000). Turn both bolts clockwise a quarter turn each, then walk on the
            belt to check that it no longer slips or hesitates. Repeat only if you have to. The manual says
            to tighten the rear roller &ldquo;only enough to prevent slippage at the front roller,&rdquo; and
            to turn the two bolts evenly, or the belt will start tracking to one side.
          </p>
          <p>
            XTERRA&rsquo;s manuals put the next warning in capitals: do not overtighten. Too much tension
            damages the belt and wears out the roller bearings early. Damage from improper tracking or
            tension adjustment is not covered by XTERRA&rsquo;s warranty, according to the TRX1000 manual.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When it is the drive belt</h3>
          <p>
            If you tighten the walking belt a lot and it still slips, the manuals say the problem could be
            the drive belt under the motor cover, which connects the motor to the front roller. A loose drive
            belt feels much like a loose walking belt. XTERRA says tightening the motor belt should be done
            by a trained service person. Our guides to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt slipping</a> and the <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill drive belt</a> explain how to tell the two apart.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A belt that stops too abruptly</h3>
          <p>
            Pulling the safety key should bring the belt to a stop with what the TR75 manual calls a one- to
            two-step coast. If it stops almost instantly, XTERRA&rsquo;s troubleshooting table blames high
            belt and deck friction and sends you back to cleaning and lubrication. It is an easy symptom to
            miss and a good early warning that the deck is dry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Drifting to One Side: Tracking an XTERRA</h2>
<p>
            The TRX1000 manual describes the tracking adjustment step by step, and other models in the range
            follow the same principle. It is an owner job: XTERRA calls proper belt tracking &ldquo;an owner
            responsibility common with all treadmills.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>Run the belt at 3 mph with nobody on it.</li>
            <li>Use the supplied Allen wrench on the left rear roller bolt.</li>
            <li>If the belt is too close to the left side, turn the bolt a quarter turn clockwise. If it is too close to the right side, turn it counter-clockwise.</li>
            <li>Wait a few minutes. XTERRA warns that a small adjustment can make a dramatic difference that is not visible right away.</li>
            <li>Repeat in quarter turns until the belt settles in the centre of the deck.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Some drift during use is normal. The manuals note that the belt can move toward one side
            depending on your gait and whether you favour one leg. The Sport series troubleshooting table
            suggests that if that walking pattern is natural, you track the belt slightly off-centre, to the
            side opposite the direction it drifts. Tracking also tends to settle as a new belt beds in, so
            expect a few small adjustments in the first weeks. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to center a treadmill belt</a> has diagrams that apply to XTERRA&rsquo;s rear-roller layout.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not try to fix a slipping belt with the tracking bolt, or a drifting belt by tightening both
            bolts hard. Tension and tracking are adjusted with the same two bolts, which is why the manuals
            insist on equal turns for tension and single-side quarter turns for tracking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lubrication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Lubrication and the LUBE Message</h2>
<p>
            <strong>XTERRA&rsquo;s stated lubrication interval depends on which document you read, so
            follow the one in your model&rsquo;s manual, and lubricate sooner whenever the deck feels
            dry.</strong> Every manual we read agrees on the method and on the warning: use only
            XTERRA-approved lubricant, and failing to clean and lubricate the deck at regular intervals may
            void the warranty.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source (XTERRA, read 24 Nov 2026)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Stated interval</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR65, TR75, TR95H and TRX1200 manuals</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every 90 days, after 90 hours of use, or earlier if dry or dirty</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRX1000 manual, text</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every 80 hours of use, or earlier if dry or dirty</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRX1000 and TR150 manuals, maintenance chart</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every 40 hours, or sooner if dry</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">XTERRA FAQ and Support Center pages</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every 3 months or 180 hours, described as current guidance</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to check and apply it</h3>
          <p>
            Switch off and unplug. Lift one edge of the belt and reach between the belt and the deck; it
            should feel slick. If it does not, the TR75 manual describes measuring about 18 inches from the
            motor cover, reaching under the belt about 4 to 6 inches from one edge and squeezing a thin line
            of lubricant in an S pattern, then repeating on the other side. Plug back in, switch on and walk
            at a moderate speed for five minutes to spread it. The TRX1000 manual uses half a bottle per side
            starting about a foot from the motor cover; amounts vary by model, so follow yours and do not
            overdo it. XTERRA lists its own <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill lubricant</a> on its FAQ page at $11.99 (24 Nov 2026).
            Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a> covers the general rules.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clean before you lubricate</h3>
          <p>
            The manuals pair lubrication with cleaning. Slide a cloth longer than the belt is wide between
            the belt and the deck, hold one end on each side, and drag it up and down the deck. Rotate the
            belt halfway and repeat until the cloth comes out clean. The TRX1000 manual also asks you to
            vacuum under the treadmill monthly and under the motor hood every other month, unplugged.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clearing the LUBE reminder</h3>
          <p>
            Many XTERRA consoles flash &ldquo;LUBE&rdquo; after a set running time. The TRX1200 manual says
            it first appears at 10 hours and then every 40 hours; pressing any button dismisses it for that
            session, but it returns each time you start the treadmill until you clear it. On the TRX1000,
            TRX1200 and TR65, the manuals say to hold the PROGRAM key for 3 seconds in standby mode to reset
            the message. The TR75 manual instead puts the lube-message reset in its Engineering Mode menu.
            The message is a reminder, not a fault: check the deck, lubricate if it is dry, then clear it. A LUBE reminder is one of six usual reasons a console beeps, all listed in our guide to <a href="/treadmill-keeps-beeping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a treadmill that keeps beeping</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-speed-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console, Speed Sensor and Incline Problems</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The console resets or the workout stops</h3>
          <p>
            XTERRA&rsquo;s pause behaviour catches people out. On the TRX1000, pressing Stop once pauses
            the belt and holds your time, distance and calories; pressing Stop a second time resets the
            console to its start-up screen, and a pause left for five minutes resets it too. The TR75 manual
            describes a pause setting that can be switched between a 5-minute limit and an indefinite pause.
            If your workout data keeps vanishing, that is probably the cause, not a fault.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed problems and E1</h3>
          <p>
            The speed sensor tells the controller how fast the front roller is turning. When the signal
            is lost, the belt may surge, stop or refuse to start, and on the TR65 the console shows E1,
            which its manual defines as a missing speed signal. The manual&rsquo;s fixes are to make sure the
            sensor is connected and, if it is defective, to replace it. On the TRX1000 family, E1 is described
            by when it appears, 8 to 10 seconds after pressing Start, with checks of whether the motor runs and
            of the console-to-controller cable. Our guide to the <a href="/treadmill-speed-sensor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed sensor</a> explains how the sensor and magnet sit on the front roller and what to look at with the hood off.
            If the belt reaches only about 5 mph whatever the display says, that is a voltage problem
            instead, covered above.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline that will not move or reads wrong</h3>
          <p>
            First, check which kind of incline you have. The TRX1000 has manual incline: you lift the deck,
            pull the pop pin, slide the incline foot to the height you want and reinsert the pin on both
            sides, before you start the belt. Nothing electrical can fail there. The TRX1200, the Sport
            series and the Hikers have powered incline, and on the TR65 a stuck or faulty incline shows as E3;
            the manual points to the cable between the incline motor and the controller and then to the
            incline motor itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some XTERRA manuals also describe a calibration that resets speed and incline limits. On the
            TR75, the manual says to remove the safety key, hold Start and Speed Up while replacing the key
            until the display shows &ldquo;Factory settings,&rdquo; and then start the calibration, which
            runs automatically. It warns that the belt will start without warning, so nobody should stand on
            it. The TR65 manual gives a different key sequence and tells owners not to change any value other
            than miles or kilometres. Use only the procedure printed in your own manual. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-incline-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline not working</a> covers the mechanical checks.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Keys that do not respond</h3>
          <p>
            The TR75 manual describes an Engineering Mode, reached by holding Start, Stop and Enter for about
            5 seconds, with a key test and a display test. If one button seems dead, the key test tells you
            whether the console is registering it. The same menu holds a child lock, which locks the keypad
            until the lock buttons are held for 3 seconds, so a keypad that ignores you may simply be
            locked. Other models may not have this menu; check your manual. The manuals also ask for light
            finger pressure on the keys, which are &ldquo;precision set.&rdquo;
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Call XTERRA and What to Have Ready</h2>
<p>
            Call when an error code survives a clean power cycle and the owner checks, when the motor,
            controller or drive belt is involved, when anything smells hot, or when the treadmill trips a
            breaker that has nothing else on its circuit. XTERRA&rsquo;s contact page (24 Nov 2026) lists
            one number, 870-336-4286, Monday to Friday 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM, for warranty and parts as well as
            sales, plus a contact form that takes up to three photos and a chat assistant. Older manuals print
            other numbers, including 800-258-8511 and a service line, 870-935-1107; use the one on the current
            website first.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Model and serial number.</strong> XTERRA&rsquo;s FAQ says the serial number is on the frame near the power cord and is 16 characters long.</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of purchase.</strong> The warranty runs from the date of retail sale, so keep the receipt. XTERRA&rsquo;s site asks owners to register at its Register Your Product page.</li>
            <li><strong>The exact code and when it appears.</strong> &ldquo;E1 eight seconds after Start&rdquo; is more useful than &ldquo;E1.&rdquo;</li>
            <li><strong>A short video.</strong> Belt noise, slipping and odd behaviour are far easier to diagnose on video.</li>
            <li><strong>What you have already done.</strong> Power cycle, circuit, lubrication and adjustments.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the warranty does not cover</h3>
          <p>
            XTERRA&rsquo;s manuals list several owner responsibilities that matter when you claim. The
            TRX1000 manual says damage from improper tracking or tension adjustment is not covered, that
            failing to clean and lubricate may void the warranty, and that the owner is responsible for a
            proper power supply and house wiring. The manuals also say low-voltage problems are not covered.
            The cheaper Compact models carry short parts and labour terms (the TRX1000 manual lists 90 days
            on parts and labour and one year on the motor), so check where you stand before agreeing to a
            paid visit. Our <a href="/xterra-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">XTERRA treadmills buyer&rsquo;s guide</a> lists the warranty on each current model, and our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a> helps decide whether an out-of-warranty fix is worth it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Finding your manual</h3>
          <p>
            XTERRA hosts owner&rsquo;s manuals for current and older models, including the TR150, TR65,
            TR75, TR85, TR95H, TRX1000, TRX1200 and TRX1400, on its Manuals &amp; Documents page. The
            TRX1200 and TR95H manuals put the serial number sticker on the frame near the power switch,
            and the manuals have a page to record it with your purchase date and dealer. Download the manual before you start: every code, interval and key sequence in this guide
            comes from those documents, and they differ enough between models to matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Simple XTERRA Maintenance Routine</h2>
<p>
            Most of the faults above trace back to friction, dirt and power. The manuals&rsquo; maintenance
            schedule, condensed, prevents most of them.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How often</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Task</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">After each use</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wipe sweat off the console and frame</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Protects the keys and display</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Monthly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Vacuum under the treadmill; check tracking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Keeps dust out of the motor and the belt centred</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every other month</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wipe the deck edges; vacuum under the motor hood, unplugged</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dust and pet hair raise friction and heat</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Per your manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clean and lubricate the deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Prevents slipping, E2 codes and breaker trips</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Whenever moved</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check level, the power cord&rsquo;s route and the outlet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Avoids cord damage and shared circuits</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two placement rules from the manuals also prevent trouble. Keep the cord away from the incline
            mechanism and transport wheels, and leave clear space around the machine; the TR75 manual
            recommends at least 20 inches at the front and sides and 6 feet behind. Do not run the treadmill
            on deep, plush carpet, which the TRX1000 manual says can damage both carpet and machine, and
            unplug it during electrical storms, as the Sport manuals advise.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Troubleshooting an XTERRA</h2>
          <p>Most XTERRA faults are simple. These four make them take longer or cost more.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Using another model’s error-code table</h3>
              <p>E1 and E6 mean different things on a TR65, a TRX1000 and a TR95H. Acting on the wrong table can mean replacing a good controller. Look the code up in your own model’s manual and note when it appears.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Tightening the belt before lubricating</h3>
              <p>A dry deck is the most common cause of slipping, E2 codes and breaker trips. Cranking the tension bolts on a dry deck wears the bearings, and XTERRA does not cover damage from improper tension. Clean and lubricate first, then a quarter turn.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Running it on a shared circuit or an extension cord</h3>
              <p>XTERRA’s manuals say the treadmill should be the only appliance on its circuit and warn against extension cords. A power strip or a shared kitchen circuit causes nuisance trips and low voltage, which the warranty does not cover.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Clearing LUBE without checking the deck</h3>
              <p>The LUBE reminder is easy to dismiss with a button press, and it will come back each time you start the treadmill. Clearing it without checking the deck lets friction build until it shows up as slipping or an E2 code.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Seat the safety key, check the front breaker and give the treadmill its
            own grounded circuit, then do a clean power cycle.</strong> Most XTERRA problems end there. If a
            code appears, look it up in your own model&rsquo;s manual, because E1 and E6 mean different
            things on different XTERRAs.
          </p>
          <p>
            For belt problems, clean and lubricate before you tighten, turn the bolts a quarter at a time, and
            track the belt at 3 mph. Call XTERRA for the motor, controller or drive belt, or for anything that
            smells hot or trips a circuit it has to itself. Our <a href="/xterra-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">XTERRA treadmills guide</a> lists each model&rsquo;s warranty, and our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">troubleshooting guide</a> covers every brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/">Treadmill Troubleshooting by Symptom</a></li>
            <li><a href="/xterra-treadmills/">XTERRA Treadmills: The Current Range</a></li>
